g=['https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bminor/glibc/glibc-2.39/malloc/malloc.c','https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bminor/glibc/glibc-2.39/malloc/arena.c'];ma=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/mallopt.3.html'];j=['https://jemalloc.net/jemalloc.3.html'];sl=['https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/slab.html'];tc=['https://google.github.io/tcmalloc/design.html']
def e(a,c,r,s=g):edit(m,a,c,r,s)
def f(n,k,c,r,s=g,o=None):qe(q,'mem-allocators-q'+str(n),k,c,r,s,o)
e('The kernel hands out memory in pages: 4 KiB at a time, via `mmap` or `brk`.','User allocators obtain virtual address ranges through interfaces such as `mmap` and `brk`; physical pages are often supplied lazily on access. This lesson uses 4 KiB pages for arithmetic, not as a universal Linux page size.','Virtual allocation is not immediate frame allocation.')
e('malloc is called millions of times a second','allocation can be frequent','Unsupported workload rate removed.')
e('Less waste, slow search','Smallest immediate remainder; search and fragmentation depend on indexing/workload','Best fit not universally less fragmentation.')
e('Allocation becomes "pop from the list for class *k*", which is O(1).','A cached fast path can be a constant-time pop from a size-class list. Refill, coalescing and requesting memory need additional work.','Not all allocations constant time.')
e('The default allocator on most Linux distros is glibc\'s, derived from Doug Lea\'s dlmalloc.','The following concrete model is **glibc 2.39 on ordinary x86-64 Linux**, with default alignment and no replacement/debug allocator. Its lineage includes dlmalloc and ptmalloc2, but glibc has substantially changed it. Other versions and tunables differ; inspect the deployed version rather than treating these defaults as permanent.','Pin implementation claims to inspected release.')
e('Each allocation is a **chunk** with an 8-byte size header just before the pointer you get back.','An ordinary arena allocation is a **chunk** with an 8-byte size/status word immediately before the returned pointer. The layout also has a preceding previous-size field; for an in-use arena chunk, usable space can borrow the next chunk\'s previous-size word. Separately mmapped chunks have different overhead.','One-word accounting not complete header layout.')
e('        void *p = malloc(n[i]);','        void *p = malloc(n[i]);\n        if (!p) return 1;','Handle malloc failure.')
e('               malloc_usable_size(p));','               malloc_usable_size(p));\n        free(p);','Release demonstration allocations.')
e('writing past the end of one chunk corrupts the next chunk\'s size field, which allocator exploits then use.','an overflow can damage adjacent payload or allocator metadata, potentially enabling exploitation despite integrity checks. Not every out-of-bounds byte reaches a size field.','Overflow impact conditional.')
e('Free chunks are kept in **bins**, checked roughly in this order:','The glibc 2.39 allocation machinery includes these structures; this is an overview, not a complete fixed search order:','Missing unsorted bin and size-dependent paths.')
e('3. **small and large bins**: doubly linked, sorted free lists. Adjacent free chunks here are **coalesced** into bigger ones to fight external fragmentation.','3. An **unsorted bin** stages many newly freed/coalesced chunks. **Small bins** hold exact size classes; **large bins** are size ordered. Eligible neighboring free chunks can coalesce; tcache and fastbin retention delay that.','Small bins not sorted variable size; add unsorted bin.')
e('4. The **top chunk**: the free space at the end of the heap, grown with `brk`.','4. The **top chunk** is an arena\'s remaining heap space. The main arena commonly grows using `brk`; non-main heaps use mappings.','Nonmain top not brk.')
e('Requests at or above `M_MMAP_THRESHOLD` (128 KiB by default, adjusted upward dynamically to at most 32 MiB on 64-bit) skip the heap. glibc `mmap`s them directly and `free` `munmap`s them straight back. That keeps big buffers from fragmenting the heap, at the cost of a system call, page faults on first touch, and a TLB shootdown on every free in a multithreaded process.','In the stated release, requests that cannot be satisfied from existing arena space may be served by a dedicated mapping when their normalized size meets the mmap threshold and mapping limits permit it. The default threshold starts at 128 KiB and can dynamically rise to 32 MiB on this model. This is not an unconditional bypass: suitable existing free chunks, settings, mapping limits and failures matter. Dedicated mapped chunks are normally unmapped on free; fault and TLB-invalidation costs depend on actual mappings and use.','Threshold eligibility is not guaranteed mmap, no all-core shootdown.',ma+g)
e('glibc gives threads separate **arenas** (up to 8 × the number of cores on 64-bit). Each non-main arena reserves its own `mmap`ed region, typically 64 MiB of address space,','glibc assigns threads to **arenas**, which can be shared (the default cap in this release/model is 8 × online CPUs). A non-main arena can acquire multiple heaps, whose maximum reservation is commonly 64 MiB in this configuration,','Not one arena per thread or one region per arena.')
e('Setting `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2` is a common fix when per-arena fragmentation inflates RSS.','Reducing `MALLOC_ARENA_MAX` can reduce retention but increase contention; choose a limit by measuring the application.','Not universal value2 fix.')
e('It is not a leak; the memory is free inside the allocator.','Allocator retention can explain this without a leak, but RSS alone cannot distinguish retention from leaked or unprofiled live allocations.','RSS cannot rule out leak.')
e('**jemalloc** (Jason Evans; FreeBSD\'s libc allocator, the default for Redis on Linux, used heavily at Meta, and the basis of Firefox\'s allocator):','**jemalloc**:',j and 'Remove unnecessary deployment claims that vary by build and product version.',j)
e('Rounding waste is bounded at about 20%.','For these classes (excluding the smallest sizes), rounding waste is bounded at about 20% of the slot.', 'Smallest-size exception.',j)
e('- Per-thread caches in front of several **arenas** (by default 4 × CPUs), with threads assigned round-robin.','- Per-thread caches in front of multiple **arenas**; the documented automatic arena limit defaults to four times CPUs, or one for a single CPU. Assignment is not a promised round-robin API contract.','Default special case and unsupported assignment.',j)
e('a front end of **per-CPU** caches','a front end supporting **per-CPU** caches or legacy per-thread mode','TCMalloc has two modes.',tc)
e('packing allocations into 2 MiB pages to cut TLB misses.','packing allocations into huge-page regions (2 MiB in the usual x86-64 example) to improve TLB use.','Platform-dependent huge page size.',tc)
e('| Fast path | per-thread tcache | per-thread cache | per-CPU cache |','| Fast path | per-thread tcache | per-thread cache | per-CPU or per-thread |','Table modes.',tc)
e('Swapping is easy: `LD_PRELOAD=/usr/lib/libjemalloc.so ./server`. Long-running, multithreaded services with mixed object sizes often see lower RSS and steadier latency; short-lived programs rarely notice.','For a compatible dynamically linked program, an installed allocator can sometimes be interposed with `LD_PRELOAD=/actual/path/libjemalloc.so ./server`. The path, ABI and allocation/deallocation boundaries must match. Static linking and some runtime/build choices require another integration method.\n\n> [!note] Performance evidence gap\n> No benchmark record accompanies this lesson, so comparative RSS, latency and cross-thread-free rankings are omitted. Measure representative workloads, retention and contention before selecting an allocator.','Unsupported performance ranking removed explicitly.',j+tc)
e('CPython\'s **pymalloc** handles objects of 512 bytes or less from pools inside larger arenas, and falls back to the system malloc for bigger ones. Lots of small Python objects therefore fragment pymalloc\'s arenas, not glibc\'s heap.','In a standard GIL-enabled build using **pymalloc**, small allocations of up to 512 bytes use pools in larger arenas, with larger requests falling back to raw allocation. Build/runtime settings matter: free-threaded CPython uses mimalloc. Extension modules can also allocate directly, so not every Python object allocation follows pymalloc.','Free-threaded/build exceptions.',['https://docs.python.org/3/c-api/memory.html'])
e('for **orders** 0 to 10 by default: 4 KiB up to 4 MiB.','over a configured range of **orders**. For an example with 4 KiB pages and maximum order 10, sizes run from 4 KiB to 4 MiB; architecture and kernel configuration determine the actual maximum.','Not universal Linux default.',sl)
e('after hours of uptime,','under sustained allocation/free activity,','Unsupported timescale.',sl)
e('Zeros in the high orders mean fragmented memory.','Zeros in high-order columns mean no free blocks of those orders in that zone; they can reflect fragmentation or simply low available memory.','Not unique fragmentation diagnosis.',sl)
e('kmem_cache "dentry" (192 B objects)','illustrative cache (192 B slots)','Actual dentry sizes vary.',sl)
e('Allocation pops a free slot; free pushes it back. No headers, no search, no coalescing, and objects of one type pack tightly.','The simple fast path takes or returns a slot. Fixed-size caches do not need a malloc-style size header on every live object, but maintain slab/free-list metadata; debugging, alignment and hardening can add per-object overhead. Refill and partial-slab selection require additional work.','Slab not no metadata or all constant simple.',sl)
e('It keeps per-CPU slabs for lock-free fast paths and lists of partially full slabs.','It uses per-CPU state and partial-slab management; synchronization and the exact fast path depend on architecture, configuration and kernel version.','Not universally lockfree SLUB.',sl)
e('User-space allocators borrowed the idea: jemalloc\'s and tcmalloc\'s per-size slabs are the same design.','User-space allocators use related grouping ideas, with different metadata, caching and reclamation policies.','Not identical design.',j+tc)
e('**Benchmarks lie.**','**Benchmarks need representative workloads.**','Benchmarks not inherently false.')
e('hits the fast path every time. Real fragmentation shows up after days of mixed traffic.','can mostly hit a cached fast path. Mixed lifetimes and sizes can expose retention or fragmentation on a different timescale.','No guaranteed path/days.')
e('and memory migrates between arenas. jemalloc and mimalloc handle this better than older designs.','and remote frees affect reuse. A glibc chunk can enter another thread\'s tcache without changing its owning arena. Performance depends on the allocator and workload.','Ownership not migrating; remove ranking.')
e('Calling glibc `free` on a jemalloc pointer corrupts the heap.','Passing a jemalloc-owned allocation to an incompatible glibc `free` is undefined behavior and can corrupt memory or crash.','Not deterministic manifestation.')
e('placement policy is their only weapon against external fragmentation.','placement, coalescing, segregating lifetimes and releasing suitable free regions help manage external fragmentation.','Only weapon contradicts coalescing.')
e('- glibc malloc uses 16-byte-aligned chunks with an 8-byte header (malloc(1) gives 24 usable bytes), per-thread tcache, bins with coalescing, per-thread arenas, and mmap for requests of 128 KiB or more by default.','- In the specified glibc 2.39 x86-64 model, ordinary chunks use 16-byte rounding, tcache and arena/bin machinery; large mapping decisions depend on existing space and thresholds.','Summary scope and mmap fixes.')
e('they often lower RSS and contention in long-running services.','their tradeoffs should be measured on the target workload.','Remove unmeasured ranking.',j+tc)
e('(orders 0 to 10); buddies','(over a configured order range); buddies','Summary configuration.',sl)
f(1,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[0]['prompt'].replace('On x86-64 Linux with glibc,','In the lesson\'s glibc 2.39 x86-64 ordinary arena-chunk model,'),'Scope exact usable-size model.')
f(3,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[2]['prompt'].replace('A driver asks the Linux buddy allocator','A driver uses one power-of-two buddy block (not alloc_pages_exact) and asks'),'Exact-page APIs can free excess tail pages.',sl)
f(5,'explanation','RSS counts resident mapped pages, not all mapped pages. The allocator can retain reusable resident memory without informing the kernel via unmap or discard.','Mapped not equal resident.',g,o=1)
f(5,'explanation','Scattered survivors prevent discarding those pages as wholly unused. They need not remain resident forever: ordinary pageable memory can be swapped. The amount retained depends on object layout.','Live not pinned resident.',g,o=2)
f(5,'workedExample','For an illustrative pool of 64-byte slots, there are 64 slots per 4 KiB page. Independent 5% survival leaves a page empty with probability 0.95^64 ≈ 3.75%, so about 96.25% retain a survivor. This is not the exact glibc malloc(64) layout: those requests normally occupy 80-byte chunks in the stated model. Surviving data prevents discarding the whole page, but does not pin it in RAM. Allocator statistics and native/runtime profiles are needed to distinguish retention from leaks. Lifetime-segregated pools, trimming and allocator changes can be evaluated; trimming cannot discard a page containing live data.','Correct glibc64 size; model math and no RSS/leak proof.')
f(6,'prompt','Which statements are true for the lesson\'s glibc 2.39 x86-64 model with its default tcache enabled? Select all that apply.','Pin version.')
f(6,'text','A 1 MiB request can receive a dedicated mmap when existing arena space cannot satisfy it and default mapping limits permit','Mmap not guaranteed by size.',o=0)
f(6,'explanation','Correct. This exceeds the initial threshold, but eligible existing free space, mapping limits and settings still affect the path.','Conditional mmap.',o=0)
f(6,'explanation','Incorrect. Dedicated mapped chunks are normally unmapped; ordinary free can cache/coalesce a chunk and may also trigger heap trimming.','Heap free can return pages too.',o=4)
f(6,'workedExample','In glibc 2.39, a tcache hit avoids arena locking. Other requests use arena machinery, including fastbins, small/large bins, the unsorted bin and the top chunk. Large requests may use dedicated mappings if existing space and mapping policy lead there. Arenas can be shared across threads. An ordinary chunk carries in-band size/status metadata. Free can cache/coalesce an arena chunk, trigger trimming, or unmap a dedicated mapped chunk.','Not size first unconditional or free only bins.')
f(7,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[6]['prompt'].replace('Ignoring metadata,','In a simplified model ignoring metadata and alignment padding,'),'Real700 roundsalignment.',sl)
f(7,'workedExample','With exactly 700-byte slots and no padding, floor(8192/700)=11, leaving 492 bytes (about 6%). A 4096-byte slab fits five and leaves 596 bytes (about 15%). Larger slabs improve packing in this example; that is not monotonic for every object size, and higher-order allocation has fragmentation and allocation-failure tradeoffs.','Not general larger slabs always better.',sl)
f(8,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[7]['prompt'].replace('What fraction of each allocation','What fraction of the allocated slot'),'Denominator ambiguity.',j)
f(8,'explanation','63/257 is about 24.5% overhead relative to requested bytes. This question explicitly asks for waste relative to the 320-byte slot: 63/320 ≈ 19.7%.','Both denominator conventions legitimate.',j,o=2)
f(8,'workedExample','The smallest listed class at least 257 is 320. Waste is 63 bytes, or 63/320 ≈ 19.7% of the slot. Above the smallest classes, the largest adjacent-class ratio in this scheme is 1.25; it is not 1.25 for every adjacent pair. Reducing a record to 256 bytes reduces its slot size from 320 to 256, a 20% reduction, assuming the same alignment and no other constraints.','Adjacent spacing arithmetic distinction.',j)
f(9,'prompt','A Ruby service using the lesson\'s glibc 2.39 x86-64 model has 64 threads and 32 online CPUs. RSS is much larger than profiled live objects, and maps show many 64 MiB anonymous reservations. Which is a plausible allocator explanation and mitigation to test?','Not diagnosis solely profiler/maps.')
f(9,'explanation','A profiler may omit native or other allocations. Stable profiled objects do not exclude every leak, and upgrading alone is not a justified fix.','Cannot ruleout leaks.',o=0)
f(9,'explanation','Arenas retain separate heap structures and can amplify fragmentation. They are not strictly one per thread, and one arena can own multiple heaps. Lowering the arena cap or testing another allocator can help or hurt depending on contention and retention.','Region/ownership caveats.',o=1)
f(9,'explanation','Stack sizes depend on limits and attributes. THP alone does not uniquely explain the observed reservations or RSS gap; inspect mapping types and allocator statistics.','Stack universal default removed.',o=2)
f(9,'explanation','In the stated release/defaults, tcache holds at most seven chunks per each of 64 small bins, about 236 KiB total per thread, not 64 MiB. Other releases and tunables differ.','Version tcache.',o=3)
f(9,'workedExample','The mappings are consistent with this glibc configuration, not proof of arena retention. The default arena cap is 8×32=256, but actual arena count is demand dependent. Threads can share arenas; chunks keep their arena ownership, although cross-thread frees can make chunks reusable through another thread\'s tcache. RSS also includes stacks, mappings and runtime/native allocations. Measure allocator statistics and memory pressure, then test an arena cap or a compatible alternative allocator against both memory and throughput.','False fixed RSS sum and no crossarena reuse absolutism.')
f(10,'prompt','In a hypothetical 48-thread image service, tracing confirms each 64 MiB scratch allocation receives a new mapping and freeing it unmaps that mapping. Page-fault and TLB-invalidation work dominate the profile. Which change directly reduces this allocation churn?','Give evidence, not threshold-guaranteed path.')
f(10,'text','Reuse a bounded scratch-buffer pool so buffers and their populated mappings can survive between requests','No guaranteed pagefault/IPI count.',o=0)
f(10,'explanation','Correct. Reuse can avoid repeated mapping, population and invalidation, at the cost of retained memory. Size the pool to concurrency and memory limits; it is not an unlimited per-thread allocation mandate.','Bound resource tradeoff.',o=0)
f(10,'explanation','The stipulated trace shows dedicated mappings. Trimming per request does not address their churn and may add work.','No universal neverheap.',o=1)
f(10,'explanation','Huge pages can change fault and translation costs but do not guarantee availability or eliminate mapping lifecycle costs.','Noallcore flush guarantee.',o=2)
f(10,'explanation','The described trace identifies allocation churn; increasing swap does not directly remove it. Inspect actual fault types rather than assuming every recorded fault is demand-zero.','No infer allminor.',o=3)
f(10,'workedExample','With an ideal page-aligned 64 MiB range and exactly one minor fault per 4 KiB page, population would require 16,384 faults. At 200 requests/s that model gives 3,276,800 faults/s. Actual malloc alignment, prior touches, huge pages and fault batching change counts; TLB invalidations need not send an IPI to every core. A 48-buffer pool retains 3 GiB of payload, so a bounded shared pool may be preferable. Reuse reduces churn but does not guarantee zero future faults or system calls.','Explicit model replaces claimed everymalloc/machinewidecount.')
f(11,'prompt','For Linux 6.8 with 4 KiB base pages, which statements about buddy and slab allocation are correct? Select all that apply.','Version and page size fixed.',sl)
f(11,'text','Many free small blocks but scarce order-9 blocks can make a 2 MiB allocation require compaction, fallback or failure','Compaction not guaranteed path/success.',sl,o=0)
f(11,'workedExample','Buddy columns report free blocks by order within each node/zone. Lack of large blocks can reflect fragmentation or low free capacity; compaction is not always permitted or successful. Linux 6.8 uses SLUB. Fixed-size cache slots avoid a malloc-style live-object size header, but metadata and debugging overhead still exist. The buddy offset is computed by XOR in the aligned model. Slab usage appears in kernel memory accounting rather than process RSS; it may nevertheless be charged to a memory cgroup. Inspect Slab, SReclaimable and SUnreclaim to understand it.','Avoid no metadata/no large runs diagnosis.',sl)
f(12,'prompt',json.loads(Path(q).read_text())[11]['prompt'].replace('ignoring pointers to the records?','ignoring pointers, page/arena overhead and pool metadata, and assuming the 20-byte record stride satisfies alignment?'),'Pool20stride not alltypes alignment.')
